  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 AM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nCurrent Overnight - Today:\n\nOngoing showers and the occasional embedded thunderstorm in \nnorthwest WI during the current overnight will gradually exit to the \nsoutheast early this morning prior to sunrise. These are occurring \nahead of a cold front currently situated in central to northeast MN. \nAdditional accumulations of largely 0.1-0.2\" or less are expected. \nBehind this, some isolated showers/decaying storms currently near \nthe southeast ND/west-central MN border track southeast during the \nearly to late morning hours, and could graze our far southern areas \nfrom the Brainerd Lakes to inland northwest Wisconsin. Little to no \naccumulations expected as the majority of them are currently \nforecast to remain just south of the Northland. We could also get \ngrazed by some isolated shower/weak embedded storm development in \ninland northwest WI this afternoon, but the potential remains around \n20% or less.\n\nOtherwise, expect some patchy fog development, some dense, through \nearly this morning as calm winds and pockets of clearing skies \ncombine with low-level moisture from recent rainfall. Fog dissipates \nshortly after sunrise today.\n\nToday will be drier and seasonal in the wake of the cold front as \nhigh pressure begins to move in, with high temperatures in the low \nto upper 70s. Northwest winds gust up to 15-20 mph this afternoon \nthen drop off quickly this evening. Cloud cover gradually diminishes \nfrom north to south this morning, though late morning-early evening \ndiurnal cumulus then create partly cloudy skies.\n\nTonight - Early Thursday:\n\nMostly clear to clear skies set up Tuesday night along with\nlight winds under high pressure in a cooler airmass. Due to\nthis, we have dropped the low temperature a couple degrees into\nthe low to upper 40s for most of the area and slightly warmer\nnear Lake Superior. The typical cold spots could touch the upper\n30s, and there is a 10% chance that those coldest spots could\nsee some frost. Wednesday night will also be on the cooler side,\nbut several degrees warmer (mid-40s to mid-50s) due to weak\novernight southerly winds and some increasing cloud cover. Also\ncan't completely rule out a 10-20% chance for a few showers in\nnorth-central and northeast MN Wednesday night into early\nThursday as weak shortwave troughing energy moves through\nnorthwest flow aloft, but low-level dry air would likely \npreclude any precipitation aloft from reaching the ground. \n\nHigh temperatures in the low 70s to around 80 F are forecast for\nWednesday, and will be a touch higher on Thursday as low-80s are\nlikely in the Brainerd Lakes east to northwest WI.\n\nThursday PM - Saturday:\n\nOur next round of scattered shower and thunderstorms are \nfavored to arrive in the Northland Thursday afternoon as a warm\nfront moves through the area and cold front starts to move in\nlate from the west. These fronts will be in association with a \nsurface low pressure moving near or through the area. Then \nadditional potential for showers and storms arrive on Friday as\nthe cold front moves through the remainder of the Northland. The\npattern at this time does not appear to favor strong to severe \nweather potential on Thursday PM or Friday, but that will depend\non the timing of the warm sector of this system (late daytime \nThursday or Thursday night) and how quickly the front moves \nthrough on Friday in relation to peak heating/instability. Since\nthis system looks to be progressive without anomalous moisture\n(PWATs only around 1-1.25\"), rainfall amounts should be on the\nmore tame side as probabilities for >0.25\" are only 30-60% and\n>0.5\" at 20-40%. Localized areas that see thunderstorms could\nsee amounts approach 1\", but widespread soaking rainfall is not\nanticipated.\n\nShowers may linger in the Arrowhead and parts of northwest WI \non Saturday as some shortwave energy aloft moves through in\nassociation with an upper-low/trough in northern Ontario, with \npeak potential in the afternoon with peak daytime heating. \nHowever, little to no additional accumulation is expected.\n\nEarly Next Week:\n\nGenerally northwest flow aloft with ridging over the western\nCONUS and troughing to the east. This could bring a return of\nperiodic rain chances through the Northland as waves of\nvorticity/energy aloft move through the northwest flow. However,\npoor agreement amongst ensemble members regarding amplitudes of\nthese waves and their timing/locations relative to the Upper \nMidwest keeps forecast confidence low in this timeframe.",
